<h2>Who buys organic cow manure from India, and why?</h2>
<p>Two buyer types make up almost all of this trade. Horticulture and garden-retail businesses in Europe and North America want a natural soil-improvement input, usually in retail-ready bags. Agricultural operations in South Asia, East Africa and Southeast Asia want bulk organic matter to build up soil that's naturally low in organic carbon. If you're reading this as either type of buyer, the sourcing conversation is largely the same — the packing conversation is not.</p>

<h2>What does "organic" actually mean here?</h2>
<p>When we use "organic manure" or "organic fertilizer," we're describing the product type — animal-origin, non-synthetic. If your buyer or your market specifically requires product certified under NPOP, USDA Organic, EU Organic or a comparable scheme, raise it before you order so we can confirm what we can supply.</p>

<h2>Which form should you order?</h2>
<p>Composted, sun-dried cow manure ships as powder, granules or cakes/pellets. Powder and granules dominate export volume because they're easier to handle, spread with standard equipment, and store without degrading. Cakes are a smaller, more traditional-use request. The form you choose affects both the container loading calculation and the price — finer processing costs more.</p>

<h2>Where the real export data points, and where it doesn't</h2>
<p>We pulled the primary WITS/UN Comtrade dataset for India's HS 3101 exports (calendar year 2023, table above) rather than relying on the trade-aggregator figures we'd previously cited for this post. It shows Nepal, the United States, the Philippines, Kenya, Bangladesh, China, Sri Lanka, Vietnam, Tanzania and Indonesia as the leading destinations by value that year — a broader and more geographically varied list than the Italy/France/USA picture our previous sourcing suggested. Two things worth flagging honestly: HS 3101 covers "animal or vegetable fertilizers" broadly, not cow manure specifically, so these figures include other products in the same customs heading; and India exported to 99 countries/regions that year in total, so the ranking reflects the largest buyers, not the only buyers. If your country isn't in the top 10 above, that doesn't mean the trade doesn't happen — it means the shipments are smaller or less frequent, which is common for a specialty soil-improvement product moving in container-load or part-container quantities rather than bulk commodity volumes.</p>

<h2>The import permit question comes before everything else</h2>
<p>This is worth raising before you get into specification or pricing conversations, because it can be a hard stop if you skip it. Animal-origin fertilizers face significantly tighter import controls in most countries than plant-origin agricultural products — many require a prior import permit, documented composting or heat-treatment declarations, or additional wording on the phytosanitary certificate. Some markets are straightforward. Others are effectively closed to this product category without the right paperwork arranged well in advance.</p>
<p>Use the IPPC's National Plant Protection Organization directory (linked in the documentation checklist above) to find your country's authority, check with them before placing an order, and share what you find with us — we'll confirm what documentation we can provide to match.</p>

<h2>Specification checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — powder, granule or cake</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture maximum</strong> — 15-25% is typical; lower moisture means more usable material per shipped tonne</li>
  <li><strong>Screening mesh</strong> — 2mm, 4mm or your own requirement, to remove stones and debris</li>
  <li><strong>NPK range</strong> — natural-material variation, confirmable via certificate of analysis per lot</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong> — bulk jumbo bags for agricultural use, or retail bags with your artwork for garden-centre programmes</li>
</ul>

